The National Park Authority undertook it's first Climate Change Risk Assessment and Adaptation Plan in 2018. The risk assessment was updated in 2025 as part of the current Management Plan review. The primary risks identified for the area, it's habitats and species and key sectors are being used to inform the climate change policies and delivery of our statutory Management Plan (2026-2031). 

The Authority produced it's first Climate Change Strategy and Action Plan in 2020. The action plan has been reviewed and updated and forms the basis of our Climate Change programme of delivery. Oversight of the Climate Change programme sits with the Authorities Policy & Resources Committee. The Committee are regularly updated on progress and involved with the annual review of the plan and it's priorities. We are currently on the 6th review cycle and the current plan runs from 2025-26. Priorities for the current Action Plan are :-

•	Work with the wider National Parks family to establish the NPAs as leaders in meeting the challenge presented by Climate Change;
•	Delivering 'Net Zero with Nature' - Linking Nature Recovery and Climate Action; 
•	Delivering Climate Action through the Planning System;
•	Supporting the Land-based sector to transition to a low-carbon future;
•	Working with our local communities - supporting them to become more resilient;
•	Working with our Local Authorities and Sector Partners to deliver positive action;
•	Support rural businesses to adapt their business operations and transition to a low-carbon economy.

column16:
The most substantial strategic decisions to date that have been influenced by climate-related risks and opportunities are the National Park Management Plan (NPMP) 2025-2030 and the Local [Development] Plan. 

The NPMP is a plan that commits the Yorkshire Dales National Park Authority (YDNPA) and its partners (collectively, the 'NPMP Partnership') to achieve specified outcomes and objectives for the national park area. Since 2019, the National Park Management Plan has included an ambition that - by 2040 at the latest - the National Park area will be: 'Resilient and responsive to the impacts of climate change, storing more carbon each year than it produces'. The 2019-24 Management Plan included 8 specific objectives to support that ambition, including objectives to increase woodland cover, restore blanket bog (peatland), reduce road haulage, and develop landscape-scale natural flood management projects.

An updated National Park Management Plan (NPMP) - for the period 2025-30 - has just been was prepared by the Partnership and adopted formally by YDNPA in June 2025. The updated Plan still contains the same ambition for climate. However, following extensive public consultation, which highlighted the scale of residents and visitors concerns about both climate and nature, the Partnership determined that the ambitions for climate and nature would be given priority ahead of the other 4 ambitions (relating to landscape, recreation, economy and community). For each ambition, the Plan contains a number of specific, time-bound objectives. For climate, these include:
* bringing all remaining degraded peatland (c28,000 hectares) into initial restoration and management by 2035;
* creating 6,000 ha of native broadleaf and mixed woodland by 2031;
* increase the take-up of natural flood management measures;
* supporting more public transport services and other measures to reduce transport emissions by 50% by 2030;
* improving the energy efficiency of buildings and increasing small-scale and community renewable energy schemes to reduce emissions for industrial, commercial and domestic sectors by 50%; 
* re-naturalising 25 km of rivers to reduce downstream flooding.

The NPM also commits the Partnership to meeting the UK Government's targets under the Protected Landscapes Targets and Outcomes Framework (PLTOF), which include climate-related targets such as peat restoration, increasing tree canopy cover, reducing total net greenhouse gas emissions and adopting nature-friendly farming practices.

Progress towards achieving the ambitions/objectives is published annually by the Partnership, and reported directly to the YDNPA Committee to allow full public scrutiny. Full details of every objective, the bodies responsible and the progress made are made public on the YDNPA's website. The NPMP objectives contain measurable targets and progress against these targets is provided as part of the reporting process.

The Local Plan sets out the policies that govern new development in the National Park. It is informed by - and contributes to the achievement of - the objectives set out in the NPMP. A new Local Plan (for 2025-2040) is currently nearing completion. Climate change is given much greater prominence in the new Plan. It includes policies that:
* require new development to demonstrate climate adaptation and low carbon measures, improving the energy efficiency of new homes and buildings; and
* steer development into the most sustainable places: nearest to existing services and social networks and away from locations that are vulnerable to flooding or other impacts of climate change

column15:
I. CREATION OF A CLIMATE CHANGE COMMITTEE AND CITY CLIMATE CORE TEAM
A. A committee under the City Development Council was established through Executive Order no. 079, Series of 2023. This is chaired by the Local Chief Executive, the vice-chairperson is a member of the City Council and the Chairman of Environment and Ecology Committee, the rest of the members include the City Planning and Development Coordinator, City ENRO, City Budget, CDRRMO, other department heads and other stakeholders. The Tagum City Climate Change Committee shall be tasked to coordinate, monitor, evaluate the programs and action plans relating to climate change. It is also tasked to:
a. Deliberate the Tagum City Promise and other Climate Change Plans;
b. Meet regularly and discuss the development plans of the Tagum City Promise and other Climate Change Plans and recommend findings to the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Tagum, for their appropriate action;
c. Deliberate and appropriately act on the work outputs and proposals pertinent to climate change adaptation and climate related risks, including the plans and investment program, related thereto;
d. Recommend key development investments in climate-sensitive sectors such as water resources, agriculture, forestry, coastal and marine resources, health, and infrastructure to ensure the achievement of sustainable development goals;
e. Perform other relevant functions to the Climate Change Adaptation and Mitigation Development;
f. Create a sub-committee to facilitate and expedite the specified task/s relating to climate change and other relevant issues; and
g. Coordinate with relevant government instrumentalities and agencies in the enforcement of policies related to Climate Change Adaptation and Mitigation.
B. A Technical Working Group was also established through the same EO. The members include representatives from the City Planning and Development Office, CENRO, City Health Office, CDRRMO, City Budget Officer, City Legal Officer, City Accountant, and other departments. The secretariat is a representative of the CPDO. The City Climate Core Team shall:
a. Formulate strategies on mitigating Greenhouse gases and other anthropogenic causes of climate change in the city of Tagum;
b. Formulate a local framework strategy on climate change to serve as the basis for a program for climate change planning, research and development, extension, and monitoring of activities on climate change;
c. Leads in the conduct of inter-sectoral analysis, validation, and reports, as well as drafting of a technical write-up of the existing situation;
d. Deliberate on the integration and finalization of studies, research findings, and consultation and survey outputs to be presented to the Committee en banc;
e. Prepare the LGU's draft of Tagum City Promise and other Climate Change Plans for the perusal and consideration of the Committee en banc; and
f. Perform all other duties and responsibilities assigned by the Local Chief Executive.
